Sorry for the slow reply, and thanks to both of you for the logs.

@MasterMT9 "The spindle speed never settled" was mostly our bug. `geo` required a coherence above 0.99 while settling, and many working drives never reach that. The limit is now 0.95, so please `git pull` and try again. Please run `python -m discprobe timing --drive E:` first and post its output, since it shows whether your GP57EB40 holds a steady speed (CAV) and how noisy its timing is.

@Sliicy Thanks for the extra detail, and I'm glad you got a good result with your parameters. I don't think USB itself is the cause, though. Several USB drives have now given usable timing, including a PLDS DS8A8SH through a SATA-to-USB adapter (`geo` result within about 0.05° rms of our SATA drive), a Panasonic UJ260AF and an LG GP65NB60. Others are much noisier, such as an ASUS BW-16D1H-U at about 200 µs. So it depends on the drive more than the connection. The [compatibility table](https://github.com/Ashley-QwQ/disc-probe#光驱兼容表) lists what has been tested so far. Your SP80NB80 may well work after the same `git pull`, if you'd like to try it again.

Your internal drive's stop at LBA 68848 (chi2/dof 505, residual 77°) is a different problem. The fit had been fine until then, so a whole-turn count was probably wrong on that long jump. That drive ran at 3590 rpm using the `flush` strategy with about 165 µs of jitter, which makes such a miscount more likely. If you have time, `timing` output from that drive would help, and so would a lower `--speed` if the drive accepts one. If you can share the drive model too, I'll add it to the table.

@MasterMT9 Thanks, that confirms it. With the MID `CMC MAG. AM3`, your drive and li-yifei's GP90NB70 measured the same disc type almost identically (u 179792.143 against 179792.142), on different drives and discs. That's a nice independent check, and I've updated the README table.

The export looks fine too: at most 0.366° from the measured map, rms 0.086°. The largest difference most likely comes from the few noisy points in your `geo`, but I can't tell which from this output.

And your picture looks great! Both figures came out clean with the same outline as the design, which is a nice result for a drive that first stopped at "the spindle never settled". Thanks for sticking with it, and I've added this to the README too.

@taniyareece7329-create 恭喜刻出来了 (๑•̀ㅂ•́)و✧ 谢谢你一路把扫描图和预设都发上来。这次 −105 帧在 aigo 上也对上了，对后面用 CD 的人很有用。

CD 不如 DVD 好看，主要是盘本身的差别。CD 的轨道间距是 1.6 µm，DVD 是 0.74 µm。轨道越密，衍射出来的颜色分得越开，所以同一张图在 DVD 上通常更鲜艳。拍照时换个角度，让灯光斜着照过来，CD 会好看不少。

方便的话发一张成品照片吧，我想把 aigo 这台光驱加进 README 的光驱表，写上你的名字。没有别的问题的话，这个 issue 我先关掉，之后需要随时再开。

@LTVA1 A Blu-ray drive, how exciting (๑•̀ㅂ•́)و✧ I'd love to see how small the features can get on BD.

Good timing too: we just made the finer grid a proper option in disc-probe, so you won't need the patch from your 23200 test anymore. `image --cell-um 10` or `--cell-um 5` draws the picture with 10 or 5 µm cells (12000 or 24000 px across the 120 mm disc), and the web page can export at those sizes too. It's only on my machine for now and goes out with the next push. I'll ping you when it's up.

Your microscope photos are the reason 5 µm is an option at all, so thank you again ✧ When the WH16NS40 arrives, tell me which discs you got and I'm happy to help set up the test patterns.

@Ashley-QwQ I tried taking some shots, hope these help.  Left and Right eye positions.  Thanks! 

