<img width="1712" height="2161" alt="right" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/be6be11a-2fed-458e-b0b6-d2da4095a394" />

@drewmedina55-ux No worries at all, and thank you for getting those photos, the patches really are shy ٩(ˊᗜˋ*)و When you come back to it, this is the part to hand to Claude:

> Test-patch result (2026-11-11, two eye positions, lamp fixed): the right view is red at the left eye and green at the right eye; the left view is blue at the left eye and nearly invisible at the right eye. So the right view leaks into the left eye. Change: give the right view a texture whose period is 10–15 % longer, so at the left eye its diffracted colour moves past red into infrared, while at the right eye it only shifts from green toward yellow. Make it a setting and put +0 / +10 / +15 % versions side by side on the next test-patch disc. Keep the random mix as the OFF texture: the fixed OFF pattern was not darker. Photograph with the lamp in the same place each time.

Happy to look at the next disc whenever it happens.

@drewmedina55-ux That was quick, thank you! An 11-byte run at 8.8 T with 81 % of its energy in that period is a good find, close to 66's 85 %.

One thing about the layout: the three rows sit at different radii, and the angle each patch sends light to also shifts a little with where it is on the disc. So if the inner row looks best, it could be the +14 % or just the inner position. Putting the three versions next to each other on the same ring (same radius, neighbouring angles) would separate the two. Or repeat each row once more at a second radius, swapped around.

About burning it here: I'll check whether we have a free slot on the drive, but I can't promise when. If you get to it first, the same two positions with the lamp in one place is exactly right.
